 "bear_case": [
  "The base-case upside depends entirely on the FCF input. The model uses $4.023B of base FCF, which is exactly the FY24–FY26 three-year average. That is about 84% above FY26 FCF of $2.184B. It also includes the FY24 peak of $6.617B, which came from a much higher-margin business (12.7% EBIT margin vs 8.3%). I rescaled the DCF to $2.184B and got roughly $22–23 a share, about a third below the $34.05 price. The 'base' 27% upside is an artifact of mean-reverting to a peak that nothing in the filings supports.",
  "FCF is falling, and the latest data points down. FCF went $6.6B, then $3.3B, then $2.2B. Q1 FY27 FCF was about -$64M (OCF $135M less capex $199M). Revenue was -4% reported and -5% currency-neutral. Management guides FY27 revenue down high single digits, and the CFO warns the actions will weigh on results into FY28. A 6% growth rate for five years applied to a normalized base does not fit a business that is still shrinking.",
  "Reported FY26 profit is flattered by a one-time item. A $986M IEEPA tariff recovery was booked in cost of sales, and $684M of it was still a receivable at year-end. Gross margin ex-recovery is about 40.8%, versus the reported 42.9% (about 190 bps lower than FY25). Operating income ex-recovery is roughly $2.8B, about a 6% margin. Pre-tax, the recovery is about 26% of reported operating income. The 16.4x trailing P/E is therefore understated, and EV/EBITDA of 11.1x is on inflated EBITDA.",
  "The dividend is not covered. FY26 dividends were $2.41B against FCF of $2.18B. In Q1 FY27 dividends were about $610M against FCF of about -$64M. Add roughly $1.0B of cash restructuring charges through FY31, $2.0B of debt due within a year, and capex that rose to $684M. Net cash is also thin or negative. Cash is $8.4B against $11.1B of debt including leases, which is net debt of about $2.7B. Without leases it is only about $0.5B of net cash. That leaves little cushion if the turnaround slips, and a dividend cut or more leverage becomes plausible.",
  "FCF quality is poor. FY26 SBC was $715M, about 33% of FCF, and FCF less SBC is about $1.47B (about 2.9% yield). Supplier-finance programs account for about $1.1B of the $3.6B of payables, which props up working capital. Receivables rose 25.7% against revenue of +0.2%. Sales reserves and the returns reserve fell while management says returns and discounts are rising, which is a margin-supportive judgment call. Hedge gains in cost of sales collapsed from $295M to $20M, and Q1 FY27 shows a $30M loss, removing a past tailwind.",
  "The moat is eroding and the turnaround is unproven. Share in sports footwear fell from 25.9% to 22.9% (Euromonitor, via a low-quality source). Greater China is -26% and Converse -28% currency-neutral. Sportswear is down low double digits and Jordan down mid-teens. On, Hoka, New Balance and Asics are growing 15–30%. Consumer switching costs are negligible, so the moat rests only on brand and scale. Management says China will get worse before it stabilizes, and Sportswear and Jordan actions extend beyond FY27.",
  "Governance and tail risks add to the downside. The Knight family controls the board through Class A, which elects 8 of 11 directors. Swoosh has said it will vote against a sunset provision, so public holders have no leverage. The CFO left within about a month of signing the 10-K, and a new COO started in December 2025. There is an unquantified Belgian customs claim with $1.3B of bank guarantees outstanding. Tax exposure is large: $953M of unrecognized tax benefits, an IRS audit, and EU State Aid. Income taxes were the auditor's critical audit matter. Securities class actions are pending.",
  "Management has destroyed value on capital allocation. About $12.1B of buybacks were done at an average of $97.57, against a price near $34. Stock options average a $96.44 strike. The shares have been through a long de-rating, and the company has repeatedly missed its own turnaround timelines."
 ],
 "assumption_challenges": [
  {
   "assumption": "Base FCF of $4.023B (a three-year average) is the starting point for the DCF",
   "why_questionable": "It is not a measure of current or near-term earning power. It mixes the FY24 peak ($6.6B) with the trough, and it exceeds FY26 FCF by 84%. Q1 FY27 FCF is negative, and FY27 revenue is guided down high single digits with EPS guided to $1.15–1.35. Using FY26 FCF puts intrinsic value below the market price. The 'reliable: true' flag and the 27% upside rest on this one choice."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "6% FCF growth for five years (base), 9% bull, 2% bear",
   "why_questionable": "The rationale itself admits low confidence and has no analyst data or guidance behind it. Even the 2% bear case starts from the inflated $4.0B base, so the bear value of $25.77 is not a true downside. The savings figure of $2.5B is cumulative through FY31, pre-charge and pre-reinvestment, which is about $0.5B a year at most. The $1.0B of cash charges is not netted against it in the DCF."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "FY26 working-capital drag is temporary, so FCF normalizes upward",
   "why_questionable": "Part of the receivable build was the $684M IEEPA item, which was collected after year-end. That is a one-off cash inflow for FY27, not a recurring run-rate. Q1 FY27 shows OCF of only $135M even after that collection, with other offsets from compensation and tax payments. Receivables ex-IEEPA still grew about 11% against wholesale revenue of +6%. Reserves fell as returns and discounts rose. That points to weaker underlying cash conversion, not a timing reversal."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "FY26 operating income of $3.8B and 42.9% gross margin represent a trough or base for recovery",
   "why_questionable": "They include a $986M one-time tariff recovery credited to cost of sales, so the true trough is lower. Management says the recovery 'largely offset' tariff costs incurred, but the gross cost is not quantified, so underlying margin cannot be cleanly stated. A recovery from a trough that may still be ahead is not demonstrated."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Nike has net cash and a strong balance sheet",
   "why_questionable": "Market Data shows debt of $11.1B against cash of $8.4B, so net debt is about $2.7B. The qualitative section cites both 'net cash' and 'leverage of about 2x'. Credit ratings were downgraded in 2025, and $2.0B of debt is current. Fixed commitments are large: $15.5B of endorsements, $4.9B of product purchases, and about $3.1B of leases."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Forward P/E of about 20x signals reasonable valuation",
   "why_questionable": "At $34.05 and the guided EPS of $1.15–1.35, the forward P/E is about 25–30x, not 20x. The source of the 20x figure is not given. It is also notable that the forward P/E (20.0x) is above the trailing P/E (16.4x), which implies analysts expect earnings to fall. Trailing earnings are flattered by the tariff recovery."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "ROE of 21.6% and ROIC of 18.7% show high-quality returns",
   "why_questionable": "ROIC subtracts all cash from invested capital and includes leases in debt, so the company's own definition flatters it. ROE is boosted by buybacks that shrank equity at prices around three times today's price. Price-to-book of 3.4x on that shrunken equity is not cheap. EBIT margin is down from 12.7% to 8.3%, and returns are heading lower."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Moat is 'narrow' and stable enough for mean reversion",
   "why_questionable": "Evidence cited for the moat (brand value above $50B, scale) is the same evidence that did not prevent a 10% revenue drop in FY25 and a 4–5% decline in Q1 FY27. Share is moving to specialists with faster growth, and Adidas is regaining lifestyle share. There is no evidence of pricing power: wholesale discounts and returns are rising."
  }
 ],
 "inconsistencies_found": [
  "The filings analysis says the stock price is $34.61 and market cap about $51.4B, while Market Data shows $34.045 and $50.57B. The filings analysis also quotes forward P/E of 20.3 and P/B of 3.45, versus 20.01 and 3.40 in Market Data. The FCF yield of 4.2% is computed on the stale $51.4B market cap. On $50.57B it is about 4.3%, and the valuation text mixes the two prices.",
  "The valuation base FCF of $4.023B is the three-year average. The filings analysis describes FY26 FCF as $2.184B, and its own growth rationale says there has been no recovery. The DCF therefore contradicts the narrative that supports it, and the base is not disclosed as an average in the notes (notes list: empty).",
  "The qualitative section says Nike 'shrank 10% in its most recent full year'. That is FY25. FY26 revenue was flat reported ($46.4B vs $46.3B) and -2% currency-neutral. The competitor growth comparison (On +30%, etc.) mixes periods and low-quality sources.",
  "Management is rated 'average' while the capital allocation notes show buybacks at three times the current price, a dividend above FCF, a paused buyback, and a CFO exit within weeks of the 10-K. The notes call net cash a plus, yet the market data shows net debt of about $2.7B including leases.",
  "The 10-K cash flow statement shows $146M of share repurchases in FY26, while the notes say repurchases have been paused since Q1 FY26 and describe 'no meaningful buybacks'. Those two statements do not fully fit unless the $146M is Q1 only or tax withholding, and the notes do not say which.",
  "Gross margin ex-recovery of 40.8% is computed ignoring the offsetting tariff costs, which the filings analysis itself flags. The same flag says it cannot be calculated. The 'one-time' label is uncertain, because tariff costs incurred are unquantified, so the true underlying margin could be higher or lower.",
  "Total debt in Market Data ($11,125M) differs from balance sheet debt plus leases ($11,072M). The model counts operating leases as debt, but FCF is already after lease payments, which double-counts leases. The market data and filings are not documented to the same basis.",
  "The market-data history lists FY2023 figures that cannot be reconciled to the provided filings, which cover FY2024–FY2026. The three-year-average base excludes FY23, but the four-year history is used elsewhere."
 ],
 "severity": "minor",
 "severity_reason": "Nothing here is fraud-level or fatal to the franchise: Nike is still the largest brand, has about 43% gross margin, an A+/A2 rating and $8.4B of cash. So I do not see a reason to avoid it regardless of price. The analysis itself has a material flaw, though. The positive valuation result depends on a $4.0B normalized FCF base that is 84% above current FCF. On actual FY26 FCF the DCF gives roughly $22–23 per share, below the $34 price. Combined with negative Q1 FCF, a dividend above FCF, tariff-inflated FY26 profit, and guided FY27 declines, the stock is not demonstrably cheap. That makes it a 'valuation-dependent' risk, not a 'major' avoid."
}
2026-10-07 22:44:48.662 [DEBUG] LLM response (stop_reason=end_turn, usage={"input_tokens":14354,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7027,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2812},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"})
{"bear_case":["The base-case DCF upside of +27% is an artifact of the starting cash flow. The code uses base FCF of $4.023B, which is exactly the 3-year average of FY24–FY26 ($6.617B, $3.268B, $2.184B). That is 84% above the latest actual FCF of $2.184B, and it leans on FY24 peak-cycle cash flow. Scaling the base case to FY26 FCF gives roughly $23–24 a share, about 30% below the $34.05 price. The same flaw lowers even the bear case: $25.77 is the 'bear' value on a $4.0B base, so the true downside is much larger.","FY27 guidance points to lower earnings and FCF. Revenue is guided down high single digits, and adjusted EPS is guided to $1.15–1.35 versus $2.10 in FY26, a drop of about 36–45%. Q1 EPS was already $0.48, so Q2–Q4 imply about $0.67–0.87 combined versus about $1.61 last year, a decline of roughly 46–58%. On guided EPS the stock trades at about 25–30x, not the optical 16.4x trailing P/E.","The dividend is not covered. FY26 dividends were $2,407M against FCF of $2,184M. Q1 FY27 FCF was about -$64M against $610M of dividends. FY27 net income at guided EPS (about $1.8–2.0B) is below the dividend run-rate. About $2.0B of debt is current, $1.0B of Pace cash charges are coming and repurchases are already paused. The choice is to burn cash, borrow or cut the dividend. A 6% FCF growth path ignores this.","FY26 operating income is flattered by a one-time item. Operating income of $3,797M is up 2.6% from $3,702M, but it includes a $986M IEEPA tariff recovery credited to cost of sales. Excluding it, operating income is about $2.8B, down about 24% year on year. Gross margin would be about 40.8%, not 42.9%. The reported 'stabilization' is not real underlying stabilization.","The reported earnings quality shows reserve and working-capital stress:\n- Receivables are up 25.7% on 0.2% revenue growth, and about 11% excluding the IEEPA receivable, against wholesale revenue up 6%.\n- Sales-related reserves fell from $1,834M to $1,589M and the returns reserve from $1,277M to $1,099M, while management admits higher returns and discounts.\n- The inventory reserve fell while Converse cites higher obsolescence.\n- About $1.1B of supplier-finance obligations sit in payables.\nReserve releases and extended terms could mean margin and OCF are overstated and may reverse.","Cash earnings are weaker than the headline FCF suggests. SBC of $715M is added back in OCF. FCF after SBC is about $1.47B, a yield of about 2.9% on a $50.6B market cap. That is thin for a business with falling revenue, share loss and a restructuring program.","Competitive share loss looks structural. Euromonitor puts Nike's sports-footwear share at 22.9%, down from 25.9%, with On, Hoka, New Balance, Asics and Adidas growing faster. Sportswear is down low double digits, Jordan down mid-teens, Greater China down 26% (EBIT -34%) and Converse down 28%. Management says China will worsen before it stabilizes, and Sportswear and Jordan actions extend beyond FY27. Consumer switching costs are negligible, and the moat rests on brand heat. 'Narrow' may be generous.","The Pace savings are smaller than they sound. The $2.5B is cumulative through FY31, about $0.5B a year on average, and it is pre-reinvestment and pre-charge. It comes with about $1.0B of cash charges, on top of FY24's $443M and FY26's $385M of 'one-time' restructuring. At a $46B revenue base, $0.5B a year is about 1 point of margin and could be absorbed by reinvestment in marketing and wholesale or by tariffs.","Governance and management risk is not priced in:\n- The CFO left about a month after signing the 10-K, and a new CFO and a new COO are in place.\n- Swoosh/Knight control means public holders elect only 3 of 11 directors and a sunset proposal will be voted down.\n- About $12.1B of buybacks were done at an average of $97.57 against a price of about $34.\nThere is little shareholder recourse if the reset fails.","Tail liabilities are unquantified. The Belgian customs claim has no loss range and no disclosed accrual, and bank guarantees rose to $1.3B. Tax exposure includes $953M of unrecognized tax benefits plus $438M of interest and penalties, an IRS audit of FY17–23 and the EU State Aid case. Fixed commitments are also large: $15.5B of endorsement obligations, $4.9B of product purchase obligations and $2.4B of other purchase obligations. These make the cost base inflexible if revenue keeps falling.","Sourcing and tariff concentration persists: Vietnam makes 52% of footwear, Indonesia 27% and China 16%, and four contract manufacturers make about 60% of production. The tariff refund is non-recurring, while the underlying tariff cost remains in FY27 COGS."],"assumption_challenges":[{"assumption":"Base FCF of $4.023B (3-year average) as the starting point for the DCF","why_questionable":"It is neither FY26 FCF ($2.184B) nor a forward estimate. It averages in the FY24 peak ($6.6B) and implies an immediate 84% rebound from a level that was still falling, with Q1 FY27 FCF at about -$64M and guided FY27 earnings down 40% or more. Using the actual $2.184B cuts the base value to about $23–24, so the +27% upside is a normalization assumption, not an output."},{"assumption":"6% FCF growth for five years (low confidence)","why_questionable":"It is applied on top of the already-normalized base, so it compounds the error. Revenue is guided down high single digits in FY27, with China and Converse still falling, and growth in Sportswear and Jordan is pushed beyond FY27. The rationale itself admits no company FCF guidance and no analyst estimates. 6% is a midpoint between scenarios, not a derived number."},{"assumption":"Working-capital reversal and IEEPA receivable collection will lift OCF","why_questionable":"This is the best bull argument, because about $684M of the receivable was collected after year-end. But it is a one-time cash inflow, not a growth driver, and Q1 OCF was only $135M even with it largely collected. The rationale concedes that compensation and tax payments offset it. Reserve declines and supplier-finance reliance suggest working capital could unwind further."},{"assumption":"Gross margin recovery (+60 bps in Q1) as evidence of turnaround","why_questionable":"The comparison is against a tariff-hit prior-year quarter. FX and hedge effects contributed +40 bps, and cash-flow-hedge gains in COGS fell from $295M to $20M and then to a $30M loss. Management expects discounts and returns to rise. Revenue is still falling 4–5%, so margin gains come from mix, FX and lower promotion on shrinking volume."},{"assumption":"Net cash and a strong balance sheet provide a safety net","why_questionable":"Total debt including leases is $11.1B against $8.4B of cash, so Nike is net debt of about $2.8B, not net cash. If leases are excluded it is roughly neutral. The qualitative section's 'net cash' and 'leverage ~2x' are inconsistent with each other. With a dividend above FCF, $2B of debt current and ratings already downgraded, the cushion is thinner than described."},{"assumption":"Trailing P/E of 16.4x and EV/EBITDA of 11x show a reasonable valuation","why_questionable":"Both rest on FY26 earnings that include the $986M tariff recovery (about $0.5 a share after tax at a ~20% rate, assuming no offsetting tariff cost). Against guided FY27 EPS of $1.15–1.35 the multiple is about 25–30x, and the 20x forward P/E is also inconsistent with that guidance. Cyclically depressed earnings on a falling trajectory mean 'cheap on trailing' is misleading."},{"assumption":"Moat rated 'narrow' and management 'average'","why_questionable":"The evidence is of a deteriorating moat, with share down 3 points, negligible switching costs and a brand reset that has taken two years without revenue growth. A CFO departure right after the 10-K, a new COO, a new restructuring program and about $12B of buybacks at three times today's price argue for 'below average' on capital allocation."},{"assumption":"Bear/bull scenarios bracket the outcomes (bear $25.77, -24%)","why_questionable":"All three scenarios use the $4.0B base. A true bear (FY26 or lower FCF, a dividend cut, 12% discount rate) gives about $14–16, down 55–60%. The bull case of $62 needs 9% growth off a base that has not been reached since FY24."}],"inconsistencies_found":["The price and market cap in the filings summary ($34.61, about $51.4B) differ from the Market Data ($34.045, $50.57B). Related multiples differ: forward P/E 20.3 vs 20.01, P/B 3.45 vs 3.40. The FCF yield, calculated at $51.4B, is stale (about 4.3% at the current price).","The forward P/E of about 20x (implied EPS about $1.70) does not fit FY27 guided adjusted EPS of $1.15–1.35, which implies a forward P/E of about 25–30x. The source of the forward multiple is unidentified, per the data gaps.","The qualitative section calls Nike 'net cash' while Market Data shows debt of $11.1B against $8.4B of cash. It also cites 'leverage of about 2x', which cannot both be true.","The base FCF used in the valuation ($4.023B, a 3-year average) is not disclosed as such, and it contradicts the growth rationale, which stresses that FCF has fallen and Q1 FY27 shows no recovery. The rationale calls the starting point a 'depressed base' while the code uses a higher, normalized one.","The growth rationale credits wholesale and North America growth as a positive, yet the filings flag wholesale as the source of higher returns and discounts, with reserves falling. Reserve releases may be what makes this growth look profitable.","FY26 operating income is shown as up slightly (3,702 to 3,797) while the filings summary acknowledges it includes a $986M one-time recovery. The 'stabilization' narrative is not adjusted for it, and the gross tariff cost the recovery offsets is not quantified, so the net effect is unknown.","The 'catalyst' of buyback resumption conflicts with FCF below the dividend and the pause since Q1 FY26. The $5.9B authorization is irrelevant without excess cash.","The ROE of 21.6% (Market Data) and ROIC of 18.7% look healthy next to an EBIT margin of 8.3%, FCF margin of 4.7% and falling earnings. ROIC excludes all cash from capital and uses an effective tax rate on EBIT, so these are flattering.","FY23 history in Market Data cannot be reconciled to the filings, so the 3-year average is only supported by FY24–FY26. The 4-year history is inconsistent with the stated 3-year basis for the base FCF."],"severity":"major","severity_reason":"The bullish valuation does not hold up. The +27% base-case upside depends on a $4.0B FCF base, 84% above the latest actual of $2.184B, and that base is itself flattered by FY24 peak numbers. On actual FY26 FCF the base case is about $23–24, below the current price. FY27 guidance implies a 40% or greater EPS decline. The dividend is not covered by FCF, and FY26 operating income includes a $986M non-recurring tariff recovery. Receivables are growing and reserves are shrinking while share is lost, the CFO has left and a new restructuring program has been announced. Nike remains a real franchise with an A+/A2 balance sheet, so this is not a solvency case. But the thesis as built rests on an unsupported FCF rebound, and the evidence is against it."}
2026-10-07 22:44:58.641 [DEBUG] LLM response (stop_reason=end_turn, usage={"input_tokens":14354,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8195,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3727},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"})
{
 "bear_case": [
  "The base-case DCF starts from $4.023B of FCF. That figure is the FY24–FY26 three-year average, since (6.617+3.268+2.184)/3 = 4.023. It includes the FY24 peak of $6.6B and is about 84% above the latest FCF of $2.184B. FCF has fallen three years in a row (6.6 → 3.3 → 2.2), and Q1 FY27 was about -$64M. Rescaling the model's own outputs to $2.184B gives roughly $23 for base, about $13 for bear and about $33 for bull (my own arithmetic, not from the filings). That puts base about 33% below the $34.05 price, and even the bull case only about matches it. The +27% upside comes from the starting point, not the business.",
  "Management guides FY27 revenue down high single digits and adjusted EPS to $1.15–1.35, versus $2.10 in FY26. That is a decline of roughly 36–45%. Q1 EPS was already $0.48, so the guide leaves only about $0.67–0.87 for the last three quarters, or roughly $0.22–0.29 a quarter. That is far below Q1's $0.48 run-rate. On guided EPS the stock trades at about 25–30x, not the 16x trailing or 20x 'forward' shown. A model that grows FCF 6% a year from an average base ignores the guidance of a steep near-term earnings drop.",
  "The dividend is not covered. FY26 dividends were $2,407M against FCF of $2,184M and net income of $3,108M. Q1 FY27 dividends were $610M against FCF of about -$64M. At the guided EPS of $1.15–1.35, the roughly $1.62 per share dividend is a payout above 100% of earnings. About $2.0B of debt is current, about $1.0B of Pace cash charges are coming, and capex is rising. The likely outcomes are a dividend cut, more debt, or a drawdown of cash, and none of these supports value. The 4.8% yield is a risk, not a cushion.",
  "FY26 earnings quality is flattered. Operating income of $3,797M is up only 2.6% year on year, but it includes the $986M one-time IEEPA tariff credit. Without it, operating income is about $2.8B, down about 24%. Gross margin would be about 40.8% versus 42.7% a year earlier, a decline of about 190 bps, not the +20 bps reported. EV/EBITDA of 11x and the 16x trailing P/E both rest on this inflated base. Adjusting EBITDA for the credit puts the multiple nearer 14x.",
  "The Q1 gross margin gain of 60 bps looks low quality. Of that, 40 bps is attributed to FX, and hedge gains in cost of sales swung from +$295M (FY25) to +$20M (FY26) to a $30M loss in Q1 FY27. Sales-related reserves fell 13% and the inventory reserve fell from $233M to $213M, even though management cites higher returns and discounts. Inventory rose 5% in Q1 while revenue fell 4%. A markdown cycle and more reserve builds look likely, which would hit margins.",
  "The moat is eroding in the data. Euromonitor puts Nike's sports-footwear share at 22.9%, down from 25.9%. Sportswear fell low double digits in Q1 and Jordan fell mid-teens. Greater China was -26%, with EBIT -34%, and Converse was -28%. Competitors are growing 13–30% (low-quality sources) in a market said to be growing about 7%. Switching costs are negligible. Only Performance, at about $16B, is growing, and it is a minority of revenue.",
  "Management and governance give public holders little protection. Buybacks of about $12.1B were done at an average of $97.57, nearly 3x today's price. The CFO signed the 10-K on July 15 and left on August 17, and a new CFO signed the 10-Q. A new $1.0B restructuring was announced two months later. This is the second large charge program in three years (FY24 $443M, FY26 $385M). The Knight family and Swoosh control Class A and elect 8 of 11 directors, and Swoosh has said it will vote against a sunset provision. An activist cannot force change. The tax profile is also uncertain: $953M of unrecognized tax benefits, an IRS audit, EU State Aid and a Belgian customs claim with no loss estimate.",
  "The Pace savings of $2.5B are cumulative through FY31, which is only about $0.5B a year. They are pre-reinvestment and come with about $1.0B of charges. The savings are small against the roughly $2.4–2.9B drop in operating income since FY24. Management also warns that actions will weigh on results into FY28. A 5-year 6% FCF growth path therefore looks too early."
 ],
 "assumption_challenges": [
  {
   "assumption": "Base FCF of $4.023B (the three-year average) as the DCF starting point.",
   "why_questionable": "It is not trailing FCF, and it is not normalized FCF either. It is 84% above FY26 FCF of $2.184B and includes the FY24 peak of $6.6B, which came before revenue fell 10% and margins compressed. The filings summary itself cites FY26 FCF of $2.18B, a 4.2% yield, and Q1 FY27 FCF of about -$64M. The valuation inputs contradict the stated facts. Using FY26 FCF cuts every scenario by about 46% before the net-debt adjustment."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "6% FCF growth for five years, described as 'moderate' and 'between recovery and stagnation'.",
   "why_questionable": "It is applied on top of an already-recovered base, so the effective recovery is much larger. Guidance is for FY27 revenue down high single digits and EPS down about 40%. China and Converse are expected to worsen through FY27, Sportswear and Jordan actions run past FY27, and Q1 revenue was -4% and OCF was $135M. The rationale itself admits low confidence and no analyst or management FCF guidance. The bear case of 2% growth also uses the inflated $4.0B base, so the downside is understated."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Discount rate of 10% and terminal growth of 3% in the base case. Bear terminal growth is 2% and bull is 3.5%.",
   "why_questionable": "A share-losing, cyclical, controlled company with a dual-class structure and a CFO change is given a 10% rate. A 3% perpetual growth rate implies it will outgrow inflation forever. Terminal value is about 73% of base value. On the scaled base each 1% change in the rate moves value a lot, so the point estimate is not robust."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The 'net cash' and strong-balance-sheet claim (moat and management sections).",
   "why_questionable": "Cash is $8.37B and total debt including leases is $11.1B, so net debt is about $2.8B. Excluding leases, debt of $7.9B is roughly equal to cash. The valuation correctly subtracts net debt, so the qualitative text contradicts the model. The same section cites leverage of about 2x. $2.0B of debt is current. There are also $15.5B of endorsement obligations, $4.9B of product purchase obligations and $2.4B of other purchase obligations, which are fixed commitments on a falling revenue base."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Management rated 'average' and the moat 'narrow', with Performance growth and Pace as catalysts.",
   "why_questionable": "The record is below average on capital allocation. Buybacks were done at about 3x the current price, the dividend is uncovered, and capex rose 59% to $684M while FCF fell. Restructuring is recurring and the CFO changed right after the 10-K. Performance growth of high single digits on about $16B (roughly $1.2B a year) is small next to declines of 10–28% in other segments. Pace savings are about $0.5B a year, pre-reinvestment."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Trailing P/E of 16.4 and forward P/E of 20.0 show a reasonable or cheap valuation.",
   "why_questionable": "Trailing EPS of about $2.08 will not repeat. FY27 guidance of $1.15–1.35 implies about 25–30x. The forward P/E of 20 implies forward EPS of about $1.70, which is above the guided range. The filings analysis also says the source of the forward P/E is not provided, so it looks stale or inconsistent with guidance. Trailing earnings also include the $986M tariff credit."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Returns on capital are high (ROE 21.6%, company ROIC 18.7%).",
